  "sections": [
   {
    "pi": "CM:009",
    "heading": "What a channel strategy is",
    "html": "<p>A <strong>channel of distribution</strong> is the path a product or service takes from the business that makes it to the customer who uses it. A <strong>channel strategy</strong> is the plan for which paths you will use, how many of them, and how you will manage the businesses along the way. For a start-up, it answers one simple question: how will customers actually get my product?</p>\n<p>There are two big choices. A <strong>direct channel</strong> sells straight to the customer with no middleman, like a bakery selling from its own counter or a 3D-printing start-up selling on its own website. An <strong>indirect channel</strong> uses intermediaries such as wholesalers, distributors, or retailers. Each one takes a share of the price, but each also gives you reach you could not afford alone.</p>\n<p>The next choice is <strong>distribution intensity</strong>, meaning how many outlets carry the product:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intensive:</strong> as many outlets as possible (bottled water, gum).</li>\n<li><strong>Selective:</strong> a limited number of chosen outlets (a salsa brand in local grocers and specialty shops).</li>\n<li><strong>Exclusive:</strong> one outlet in an area (a premium cookware line sold by one boutique per city).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"example\"><h4>Example</h4><p>Dana makes hot sauce. At first she sells only at farmers markets (direct). Later she places bottles in six local specialty stores (indirect, selective). A strategy ties these together: where the sauce is sold, who handles it, and how she keeps the brand image strong.</p></div>\n<p>A good strategy also plans for <strong>channel conflict</strong>. If Dana sells on her own website for less than the stores charge, her store partners will be upset. Strategy means setting prices, territories, and rules so the channels support each other instead of competing.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><p>Remember the three levels of strategy: direct or indirect, how many outlets (intensive, selective, exclusive), and how the members work together.</p></div>"
   },
   {
    "pi": "CM:010",
    "heading": "How to select channels",
    "html": "<p>Choosing channels is a decision, not a guess. A manager looks at several factors and then matches the channel to them.</p>\n<h4>Factors that drive the choice</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target market:</strong> Where do your customers already shop? Teens and busy parents may buy online, while older neighbors may prefer a local store.</li>\n<li><strong>Product:</strong> Perishable, fragile, custom, or high-priced items often need shorter, more direct channels. Low-cost everyday items usually need wide, intensive distribution.</li>\n<li><strong>Company resources:</strong> A start-up with little cash may not be able to run its own stores, so an indirect channel can be the smarter way to reach customers.</li>\n<li><strong>Control:</strong> Direct selling gives the most control over price, image, and customer service. Every intermediary gives some of that up.</li>\n<li><strong>Costs:</strong> Compare the margin each channel member takes with the sales it brings in.</li>\n<li><strong>Competition:</strong> Where do rivals sell? Sometimes you match them, and sometimes you pick a different path to stand out.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"example\"><h4>Example</h4><p>A cleaning service sells a service, which cannot be stored on a shelf. It will almost always use a direct channel: the crew goes to the customer. A start-up that makes dog treats, on the other hand, might sell online (direct) and through pet stores (indirect) to reach more buyers.</p></div>\n<p>Many start-ups use <strong>multiple channels</strong>, such as a website plus a few retailers, to reach more people. Using more than one adds reach but also adds work and the risk of conflict. A smart choice starts small, tests one or two channels, and measures sales, cost per sale, and customer feedback before adding more.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><p>The best channel is the one your target customers will actually use that you can afford and still control well enough to protect your brand.</p></div>"
   },
   {
    "pi": "CM:011",
    "heading": "Evaluating channel members",
    "html": "<p>Once you pick channels, you must keep checking the businesses that carry your product. A channel member that sells poorly, treats customers badly, or damages your image can hurt a start-up fast. <strong>Evaluating channel members</strong> means measuring how well each one is doing its job.</p>\n<h4>What to evaluate</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sales performance:</strong> units sold, revenue, and growth compared with goals.</li>\n<li><strong>Customer service:</strong> complaints, returns, and how well staff explain and support your product.</li>\n<li><strong>Reliability:</strong> on-time ordering and delivery, and keeping shelves stocked.</li>\n<li><strong>Financial health:</strong> pays invoices on time and is not close to going out of business.</li>\n<li><strong>Brand fit:</strong> the store, display, and staff match the image you want.</li>\n<li><strong>Cooperation:</strong> shares sales data, follows agreements, and communicates openly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A simple tool is a <strong>scorecard</strong>. List the criteria, rate each member from one to five, and compare them at regular times such as every quarter.</p>\n<div class=\"example\"><h4>Example</h4><p>Rico sells phone cases through three gift shops. Shop A sells 120 cases a quarter and pays on time. Shop B sells 40 and often pays late. Shop C sells 90 but shows the cases in a dusty corner. Rico keeps Shop A, works with Shop C on a better display, and decides whether to drop Shop B.</p></div>\n<p>The results guide action: reward top performers with better terms, coach average ones, and end agreements with members that keep missing standards. Clear written expectations at the start make this fair and easier.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><p>Evaluation looks at both numbers (sales, payment) and quality (service, image). A member with good sales but a poor image may still be a bad fit.</p></div>"
   }
  ],
