  "sections": [
   {
    "pi": "MP:004",
    "heading": "Identifying market segments",
    "html": "<p>No start-up can serve everyone. <strong>Market segmentation</strong> is dividing a large market into smaller groups of people who share needs, traits, or buying habits. Once you see the groups, you can choose the ones you serve best.</p>\n<h4>Four common ways to segment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Demographic:</strong> age, gender, income, education, family size, occupation.</li>\n<li><strong>Geographic:</strong> city, neighborhood, climate, urban or rural.</li>\n<li><strong>Psychographic:</strong> lifestyle, values, interests, personality.</li>\n<li><strong>Behavioral:</strong> how often they buy, how loyal they are, what benefits they want, and how they use the product.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"example\"><h4>Example</h4><p>A new laundromat looks at its neighborhood. Segments include college students (demographic, tight budgets), young families (large loads, want fast machines), and apartment renters without machines at home (geographic and behavioral). Each group wants something different: cheap wash cards, free folding tables, or pickup and delivery.</p></div>\n<p>A usable segment must be <strong>measurable</strong> (you can estimate its size), <strong>reachable</strong> (you can advertise to it), <strong>big enough</strong> to be profitable, and <strong>different</strong> from other segments in how it responds. If two groups act the same, they are one segment.</p>\n<p>Start-ups often combine bases. \"Busy parents under 40 who live within three miles and order food online weekly\" uses demographic, geographic, and behavioral traits together.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><p>Remember D-G-P-B: Demographic, Geographic, Psychographic, Behavioral. Needs-based thinking beats guessing: group people by what they want, then describe them with these four bases.</p></div>"
   },
   {
    "pi": "MP:031",
    "heading": "Developing a customer profile",
    "html": "<p>A <strong>customer profile</strong> is a detailed description of a typical buyer in a segment. It is often written as a <strong>persona</strong>: a made-up but realistic person built from real data. It helps everyone on the team picture who they are serving.</p>\n<h4>What a strong profile includes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Demographics:</strong> age, income, job, household.</li>\n<li><strong>Psychographics:</strong> values, interests, and lifestyle.</li>\n<li><strong>Needs and problems:</strong> what the customer is trying to solve.</li>\n<li><strong>Buying behavior:</strong> where they shop, how often, how much they spend, and what influences them.</li>\n<li><strong>Media habits:</strong> which social sites, local news, or search tools they use.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"example\"><h4>Example</h4><p>A food truck serving office workers builds \"Office Olivia\": age 32, works in an accounting firm downtown, has a 30-minute lunch break, wants healthy food under $12, follows local food trucks on social media, and orders ahead on her phone. The truck now knows to park near office buildings at 11:30, offer a quick pre-order option, and post the daily location online.</p></div>\n<p>Profiles must come from evidence, not guesses: surveys, interviews, sales records, website data, and watching customers. They should be updated as the business learns more. A profile is a tool for decisions about product, price, place, and promotion.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><p>A segment is a group; a customer profile is one detailed picture of a typical member of that group.</p></div>"
   },
   {
    "pi": "MP:005",
    "heading": "Selecting a target market",
    "html": "<p>The <strong>target market</strong> is the specific segment (or segments) a business chooses to serve. Selecting it is one of the most important marketing choices a start-up makes, because every other decision, from price to promotion, follows from it.</p>\n<h4>Criteria for choosing</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Size and growth:</strong> enough buyers, and growing.</li>\n<li><strong>Fit:</strong> matches the business's skills and resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Profit potential:</strong> buyers can afford and are willing to pay.</li>\n<li><strong>Accessibility:</strong> you can reach them with your promotion and distribution.</li>\n<li><strong>Competition:</strong> the group is not already perfectly served by stronger rivals.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Three target-market strategies</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Undifferentiated (mass):</strong> one offer for the whole market. Rare for start-ups.</li>\n<li><strong>Concentrated (niche):</strong> focus on one segment. Common for start-ups because it needs fewer resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Differentiated:</strong> separate offers for several segments. Needs more money and staff.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"example\"><h4>Example</h4><p>A 3D-printing start-up could serve hobbyists, engineers, and schools. With one printer and one employee, it picks a niche: custom tabletop-game miniatures for hobbyists. This choice shapes its products, its prices, and its social media. Later, when cash grows, it may add schools as a second target.</p></div>\n<p>A clear target also tells you who you are <em>not</em> serving, which keeps the budget focused. Targeting too broadly spreads a small start-up too thin.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><p>Segment first, then profile, then select. Start-ups usually win by choosing a niche they can serve better than anyone else.</p></div>"
   }
  ],
