   {
    "pi": "PM:209",
    "heading": "Building a product or service brand",
    "html": "<p>A <strong>brand</strong> is the name, look, and promise that tells customers who you are and what to expect. Branding is more than a logo. It is the whole picture customers carry in their minds, shaped by the product, the service, and the message.</p>\n<h4>Building blocks of a brand</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Brand name:</strong> short, easy to say and spell, and fitting the offering.</li>\n<li><strong>Logo and design:</strong> colors, typeface, and packaging that are recognizable and consistent.</li>\n<li><strong>Tagline or slogan:</strong> a short phrase that states the promise.</li>\n<li><strong>Brand voice:</strong> the personality of the writing and speaking (fun, calm, expert).</li>\n<li><strong>Brand promise:</strong> what customers can always count on.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Steps to build the brand</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Know the target market and the USP.</li>\n<li>Choose a brand personality that fits them.</li>\n<li>Create the name, logo, and message.</li>\n<li>Deliver the promise every time, since the experience makes the brand real.</li>\n<li>Stay consistent across the website, signs, packaging, social media, and staff behavior.</li>\n<li>Protect it, for example by registering a trademark.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"example\"><h4>Example</h4><p>A start-up makes eco-friendly dog toys. It names the brand \"Pawprint Green,\" uses earthy colors, packages toys in recycled paper, and promises \"Safe for pups, kind to Earth.\" Each of these choices supports the same idea. Customers soon link the brand with safe, sustainable pet products.</p></div>\n<p>Over time, a strong brand builds <strong>brand awareness</strong>, <strong>brand loyalty</strong>, and <strong>brand equity</strong>, the extra value a known, trusted name adds. Services need branding too, but because they are intangible, branding relies more on people, process, and reputation.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><p>Brand = promise + identity + experience. A good logo with a poor experience does not make a good brand.</p></div>"
   },
   {
    "pi": "PM:126",
    "heading": "Building corporate brands",
    "html": "<p>A <strong>corporate brand</strong> is the identity of the whole company, not just one product. It represents the company's values, reputation, and character, and it influences how customers, employees, suppliers, lenders, and the community view everything the company does. At the owner level, building the corporate brand is a strategic job.</p>\n<h4>Product brand versus corporate brand</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>product brand</strong> names a specific offering (one flavor, one service package).</li>\n<li>A <strong>corporate brand</strong> is the company name that stands behind them all.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Brand architecture choices</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Branded house:</strong> one master brand covers everything. Example: a business that sells \"Summit\" 3D printers, \"Summit\" filament, and \"Summit\" design services. Trust and awareness transfer across all lines, and marketing costs are shared.</li>\n<li><strong>House of brands:</strong> separate brand names for different products, with the company in the background. This protects each brand if another has trouble, but costs more to market.</li>\n<li><strong>Hybrid:</strong> product names plus the company name (\"Summit Studio\").</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>How owners build a corporate brand</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Define the <strong>mission, vision, and values</strong> and let them guide decisions.</li>\n<li>Keep <strong>consistent identity</strong> (name, logo, tone) everywhere.</li>\n<li>Deliver <strong>quality and service</strong> across all products, since one bad line can hurt the whole name.</li>\n<li>Build <strong>reputation</strong> through ethics, community involvement, fair treatment of employees, and honest communication.</li>\n<li>Align employees, because they represent the brand daily.</li>\n<li>Monitor reputation and protect the brand legally.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"example\"><h4>Example</h4><p>A neighborhood bakery grows into a bakery, a café, and a catering service. The owner uses one corporate brand, \"Hearth and Honey,\" with the same warm colors and a promise of \"local, fresh, friendly.\" Each new line benefits from the trust already earned, as long as quality stays consistent.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tip\"><p>Owners ask: does this new product fit the brand? If it could damage the corporate reputation, either improve it or give it a separate name.</p></div>"
   }
